1
00:00:01,730 --> 00:00:05,785
Hi again, there are a few key ways
that project managers help build high-

2
00:00:05,785 --> 00:00:09,830
functioning teams who work
together to meet project goals.

3
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:13,190
They create systems that
turn chaos into order;

4
00:00:13,190 --> 00:00:17,482
They communicate and listen; they
promote trust and psychological safety;

5
00:00:17,482 --> 00:00:21,080
they demonstrate empathy and
create motivation;

6
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:23,656
they delegate responsibility and
prioritize; and

7
00:00:23,656 --> 00:00:26,040
they celebrate team's success.

8
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,380
Let's go through each of
those points one by one.

9
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:33,572
Good project managers lead their teams
by creating systems that turn chaos

10
00:00:33,572 --> 00:00:35,170
into order.

11
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:39,286
You can do this by creating,
implementing, and improving: standardize,

12
00:00:39,286 --> 00:00:43,680
measurable, repeatable and scalable
workflows and processes for your team.

13
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:48,336
For example, if you find that you're
usually chasing down teammates for

14
00:00:48,336 --> 00:00:51,294
progress on their work,
you might set up a process for how and

15
00:00:51,294 --> 00:00:54,600
when a teammate should let you know
that they've completed a task.

16
00:00:55,750 --> 00:00:59,720
Think of creating systems as
a kind of connect the dots puzzle.

17
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:03,200
Being a project manager is sort of
like that, but there are no numbers,

18
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,720
just dots and a partial image.

19
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:07,800
But we don't need the numbers.

20
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,207
We can notice the dots,
know their importance and

21
00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:13,720
string them together to help everyone
visualize the project as a whole.

22
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:18,585
You find the system through the chaos and
help other people to find it, too.

23
00:01:19,955 --> 00:01:23,917
Another way that project managers lead
is through communicating and listening.

24
00:01:23,917 --> 00:01:27,563
As the project manager, it's your job
to ensure that everyone on your team

25
00:01:27,563 --> 00:01:31,365
is on the same page regarding
the status of your project.

26
00:01:31,365 --> 00:01:34,646
For example, you might communicate
with the team via daily or

27
00:01:34,646 --> 00:01:38,680
weekly status update emails and
regular team meetings.

28
00:01:38,680 --> 00:01:41,220
Team meetings also provide a space for
listening,

29
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:44,080
whether you're gathering feedback
on a workflow or process, or

30
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,420
fielding questions from the group.

31
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:49,762
You can also solicit feedback or
questions via status update emails,

32
00:01:49,762 --> 00:01:53,059
since some people might feel more
comfortable speaking in private.

33
00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:57,588
In addition to communicating and
listening to the wider team,

34
00:01:57,588 --> 00:02:02,350
it's also your responsibility to regularly
connect with individual teammates.

35
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:05,776
You do this by gaining an understanding
of communication styles and

36
00:02:05,776 --> 00:02:09,560
by asking people on your team
how they prefer to communicate.

37
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:13,220
What's important to know is that
everyone communicates differently.

38
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:17,560
For example, I might make small talk
with colleagues who I know enjoy it.

39
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:20,390
Or I might get straight to the point
with colleagues who prefer not to chat.

40
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:26,004
Project managers also lead by promoting
trust and psychological safety within

41
00:02:26,004 --> 00:02:27,630
their team.

42
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:32,061
As a reminder, psychological safety
refers to an individual's perception of

43
00:02:32,061 --> 00:02:34,979
the consequences of taking
an interpersonal risk.

44
00:02:36,190 --> 00:02:40,448
Team members need to trust that they're
safe and speaking up if they have feedback

45
00:02:40,448 --> 00:02:45,020
or concerns about the project as a whole
or about their individual tasks.

46
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:49,509
It's your job to create a team atmosphere
where different opinions are welcome, and

47
00:02:49,509 --> 00:02:53,890
all members remain respectful of one
another during challenging conversations.

48
00:02:55,110 --> 00:02:58,829
For example, during weekly status
meetings, you might schedule time for

49
00:02:58,829 --> 00:03:02,070
open, thoughtful and
inclusive discussions.

50
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:03,701
You might model this by asking for

51
00:03:03,701 --> 00:03:07,190
help solving a problem
that's impacting the team.

52
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:10,644
You should encourage contributions
from all team members regardless of

53
00:03:10,644 --> 00:03:12,200
role or rank.

54
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:15,578
By doing this, you make clear to
your teammates that they should feel

55
00:03:15,578 --> 00:03:18,840
comfortable challenging processes and
workflows.

56
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:22,632
And you make clear that critiques of
the project and plans are welcomed and

57
00:03:22,632 --> 00:03:25,120
valued no matter who they come from.

58
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:28,220
While it can feel challenging
to provide and accept feedback,

59
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:31,610
it's a healthy part of project
management and usually makes for

60
00:03:31,610 --> 00:03:33,060
a better project outcome.

61
00:03:34,310 --> 00:03:38,504
On an individual level, project
managers lead by practicing empathy and

62
00:03:38,504 --> 00:03:41,720
creating motivation for
their team members.

63
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:45,890
It's possible you've heard the phrase,
"there's no I in team,"

64
00:03:45,890 --> 00:03:49,611
and while that's true that teams need
to work together to be effective,

65
00:03:49,611 --> 00:03:54,003
it's also important to remember that teams
are made up of individuals with differing

66
00:03:54,003 --> 00:03:57,070
motivations and lives outside of work.

67
00:03:57,070 --> 00:04:01,254
You can demonstrate empathy for your
teammates by being present, listening, and

68
00:04:01,254 --> 00:04:02,970
asking questions.

69
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:04,554
During one on one conversations,

70
00:04:04,554 --> 00:04:09,000
avoid making assumptions about what
the other person is thinking and feeling.

71
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,080
If you're quiet and curious,
there's always more to learn.

72
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:16,968
In addition to showing empathy for my
team, I also like to create motivation by

73
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:20,110
recognizing a job well done
through public forums.

74
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:21,830
Like in a meeting or a group email.

75
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:26,056
Recognition tells people that
they're doing the right things, and

76
00:04:26,056 --> 00:04:28,850
motivates them to keep up the good work.

77
00:04:28,850 --> 00:04:32,110
Be sure to recognize good work,
and not just heroic efforts.

78
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:38,160
Project managers also lead by delegating
responsibility and prioritizing.

79
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:43,560
Most projects will likely have multiple
tasks taking place simultaneously.

80
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:47,376
And it's your job to keep the team focused
and heading toward the project goals and

81
00:04:47,376 --> 00:04:48,660
deliverables.

82
00:04:48,660 --> 00:04:52,913
By delegating responsibility for specific
tasks to individuals on your team,

83
00:04:52,913 --> 00:04:55,641
you provide your teammates
with the opportunity to

84
00:04:55,641 --> 00:04:59,190
add value using their
particular set of skills.

85
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:02,380
You also give yourself space to
focus on the project as a whole.

86
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:06,946
By prioritizing tasks, you reduce
ambiguity and provide clarity for

87
00:05:06,946 --> 00:05:08,190
your team.

88
00:05:08,190 --> 00:05:11,170
If you think that a task is important,
but your team doesn't,

89
00:05:11,170 --> 00:05:13,940
they might work on
whatever tasks they like.

90
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,565
By prioritizing and
making that prioritization known,

91
00:05:17,565 --> 00:05:19,156
you keep the team focused.

92
00:05:19,156 --> 00:05:22,691
Work with your team to build consensus
around priorities, explaining your

93
00:05:22,691 --> 00:05:26,623
rationale can help you get their buy-in
and increase their commitment to the work.

94
00:05:26,623 --> 00:05:30,068
Lastly, project managers lead
by celebrating team success,

95
00:05:30,068 --> 00:05:33,640
both at the end of the project and
throughout it.

96
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:37,468
This includes celebrating big and
small wins like reaching a milestone or

97
00:05:37,468 --> 00:05:40,135
receiving positive feedback
from stakeholders.

98
00:05:40,135 --> 00:05:42,491
Celebrating success is
an important tool for

99
00:05:42,491 --> 00:05:46,900
motivating the team because it increases
morale and boosts the team's engagement.

100
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:52,227
You might celebrate the team with
a group lunch, a small gift or

101
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:54,990
even just a congratulatory email.

102
00:05:54,990 --> 00:05:59,370
Simple gestures like these demonstrate
appreciation for the team's hard work.

103
00:05:59,370 --> 00:06:02,114
And when people feel appreciated,
they tend to work harder and

104
00:06:02,114 --> 00:06:03,420
their teams perform better.

105
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:09,653
To recap, project managers build high
functioning teams in a few key ways:

106
00:06:09,653 --> 00:06:15,370
creating systems that turn chaos into
order; communicating and listening;

107
00:06:15,370 --> 00:06:19,547
promoting trust and psychological safety;
demonstrating empathy and

108
00:06:19,547 --> 00:06:23,793
creating motivation; delegating
responsibility and prioritizing; and

109
00:06:23,793 --> 00:06:25,652
celebrating team success.

110
00:06:25,652 --> 00:06:30,536
As you grow in your career, you'll
strengthen your ability to lead bigger and

111
00:06:30,536 --> 00:06:36,030
more complex teams, but the principles
of teamwork will remain the same.

112
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:39,191
Coming up, you'll learn about
the stages of team development and

113
00:06:39,191 --> 00:06:41,020
how to manage team dynamics.

114
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:41,620
I'll see you soon.
